WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.115
If you're building multi-agent systems, schema design for inter-agent communication is one of the

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:08.636
places where reliability is won or lost.

3
00:00:08.699 --> 00:00:15.600
In this lesson, we'll look at why unstructured handoffs fail, what a well-formed handoff message needs to include,

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.392
how to exclude content that inflates cost without adding value, and how to version and test schemas

5
00:00:22.392 --> 00:00:24.931
as your agent implementations change.

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:32.200
A sending agent knows why it's asking for something. The receiving agent knows only what showed up in the message.

7
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:39.299
Everything that closes that gap is the handoff message. A structured payload carrying the task description,

8
00:00:39.299 --> 00:00:45.299
the context the receiver needs, the expected output format, and any constraints on the work.

9
00:00:45.299 --> 00:00:49.599
The reason it has to be that deliberate is context isolation.

10
00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:56.000
Subagents live in their own context window, and nothing crosses that boundary on its own.

11
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.089
If it wasn't included, the receiver doesn't have it. Schema versioning is the practice of tagging

12
00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:04.537
that payload with a version identifier,

13
00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:10.500
so receivers keep parsing correctly as the format evolves over time.

14
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.199
Freeform handoffs fail in three consistent ways. Missing context is the most common.

15
00:01:16.199 --> 00:01:22.186
The sending agent assumed the receiver already knew something it doesn't, and the receiver can't act

16
00:01:22.186 --> 00:01:25.240
correctly because a critical fact was never passed.

17
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:33.000
Inconsistent format means there's no agreed structure, so the receiver is parsing raw text and guessing at intent.

18
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.099
Implicit assumptions are the most dangerous. The sender's model of the receiver is wrong,

19
00:01:38.099 --> 00:01:43.300
and neither agent has a way to surface that mismatch until something breaks downstream.

20
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.500
Structured schemas address all three. They make the expected content explicit,

21
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:54.584
establish a shared format both sides can rely on, and force the sender to think through what the

22
00:01:54.584 --> 00:01:57.436
receiver actually needs rather than assuming.

23
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:04.258
Every reliable handoff message has four components. The task description tells the receiver what

24
00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:06.229
it's being asked to produce.

25
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:13.100
Relevant context is the subset of facts the receiver needs to do that, with no extras.

26
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:19.100
The output format specification tells the receiver exactly what structure the result should take.

27
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:25.300
A JSON object, a list, a specific field layout, and constraints define the boundaries,

28
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:30.899
scope limits, and any safety or policy restrictions the receiver has to respect.

29
00:02:30.899 --> 00:02:37.789
These four elements together give the receiving agent everything it needs to act without requiring

30
00:02:37.789 --> 00:02:42.429
any access to the sender's internal state or conversation history.

31
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.899
Deciding what to include and what to exclude is where schema design actually pays off.

32
00:02:47.899 --> 00:02:55.600
What to include is the task description, the relevant context, the output format spec, and the explicit constraints.

33
00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.300
If the receiver can't act without something, it goes in. What to exclude is everything else.

34
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:07.699
Full conversation histories, sender deliberation logs, internal state, redundant context.

35
00:03:07.699 --> 00:03:10.899
Excess content doesn't improve the receiver's performance.

36
00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:17.100
It inflates token cost, introduces noise, and makes the schema harder to parse.

37
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.300
The guiding question is whether the receiver needs this to complete the task.

38
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:27.600
If the answer is no, leave it out. Minimal but sufficient is the design target.

39
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.899
Two failure patterns sit on opposite ends of the over-specification problem.

40
00:03:31.899 --> 00:03:37.100
Under-specified handoffs leave the receiver without enough context to act.

41
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:44.100
The receiver fills in the gaps with guesses, which leads to hallucinations, wrong outputs, or silent failure.

42
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.699
Over-specified handoffs dump the full sender context on the receiver.

43
00:03:48.699 --> 00:03:54.500
The receiver technically has everything it needs, but it also has a lot it doesn't need.

44
00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:02.199
That excess content increases cost, slows processing, and creates noise that competes with the signal.

45
00:04:02.199 --> 00:04:08.000
Good schema design threads the needle. Enough context to act, nothing beyond that.

46
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.300
The test is whether the receiver can succeed using only what was passed, with nothing left to assume.

47
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.700
Schemas need to evolve without breaking the agents that consume them.

48
00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.299
The core practice is version tagging.

49
00:04:22.299 --> 00:04:28.799
Every handoff message carries a schema version field the receiver can inspect to know how to parse the payload.

50
00:04:28.899 --> 00:04:33.200
Beyond that, receivers should handle missing optional fields gracefully,

51
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.200
rather than crashing when a field they expect isn't present.

52
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.000
New required fields need a migration path.

53
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.000
You can't add a required field to a schema without also updating every sender that produces messages in that format.

54
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Forward compatibility means the receiver can process messages from older senders even after the schema has evolved.

55
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.500
That's what keeps a multi-agent system stable during incremental updates,

56
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.299
rather than requiring full system deploys for every schema change.

57
00:05:03.299 --> 00:05:08.899
Schema testing should happen before the receiver runs in production, not after it fails.

58
00:05:08.899 --> 00:05:15.500
Contract tests verify that every required field is present in the handoff message before it's dispatched.

59
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:20.600
Simulating the receiver with the schema alone, without actually running the full agent,

60
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:25.100
lets you check whether the message is sufficient to produce the expected output.

61
00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:30.100
Testing with intentionally missing fields confirms the receiver degrades gracefully,

62
00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.000
rather than crashing or silently skipping work.

63
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.899
A schema test suite is the simplest form of inter-agent integration testing.

64
00:05:37.899 --> 00:05:41.600
It lets you catch handoff mismatches at development time,

65
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.000
rather than diagnosing silent failures in a live system.

66
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:52.000
The cost of writing these tests is small compared to the cost of debugging a broken handoff in production.

67
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.100
Schemas that are tightly coupled to a specific agent implementation are fragile.

68
00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:02.000
If the schema contains fields that only one particular receiver knows how to interpret,

69
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.700
then you can't swap in a different implementation without rewriting the schema and every sender that produces it.

70
00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.299
The right approach is to design schemas against an interface.

71
00:06:12.299 --> 00:06:16.100
Specify what the task needs and what the output should look like,

72
00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.500
without encoding assumptions about which specific agent will do the work.

73
00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:23.799
Receivers that share a schema should be interchangeable.

74
00:06:23.799 --> 00:06:26.299
That's the property of substitutability,

75
00:06:26.299 --> 00:06:31.799
and it's what lets you swap an agent implementation for a newer or more capable version,

76
00:06:31.799 --> 00:06:35.399
without breaking the rest of the system.

77
00:06:35.399 --> 00:06:41.000
Structured handoff schemas are what separate brittle multi-agent systems from reliable ones.

78
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.899
Include what the receiver needs to act on.

79
00:06:43.899 --> 00:06:45.899
Leave out everything else.

80
00:06:45.899 --> 00:06:50.000
Version the schema so it can evolve without breaking older senders,

81
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.399
and write contract tests that catch mismatches at development time instead of in production.

82
00:06:56.399 --> 00:07:00.899
Design against an interface rather than one specific receiver,

83
00:07:00.899 --> 00:07:05.000
and you keep the freedom to swap implementations later.

84
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.600
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

